---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout>
  <main class="flex flex-col w-full justify-center items-center">
    <div class="w-1/2 flex flex-col gap-10 my-[10vh]">
      <div class="header flex flex-col gap-5">
        <h1 class="text-4xl">Research</h1>
        <p>
          Explore the research done on net neutrality and the digital divide.
        </p>
      </div>
      <pre>
        [ NET NEUTRALITY & THE DIGITAL DIVIDE ]
      
        +-----------------------------------+
        |        FREE & OPEN INTERNET       |
        |         (Packet Equality)         |
        |                                   |
        |  [=]  [=]  [=]  [=]  [=]  [=]   | &lt;--- All data treated the same
        |                                   |
        +-----------------------------------+
                  |           |
                  |           |  ISPs create tolls/fast lanes
        +-----------------------------------+
        |        THE DIGITAL DIVIDE         |
        |       (Economic Redlining)        |
        |                                   |
        |       $$$           ...           |
        |    [======]       [===]           |
        |    High Speed     Low Speed       |
        |   (Fiber)         (Broken/Throttled)|
        |                                   |
        |    [======]       [===]           |
        |   Wealthy Area   Under-invested   |
        |                                   |
        +-----------------------------------+
      </pre>
      <div class="research-papers flex flex-col gap-25">
        <div class="flex flex-col gap-4">
          <h3 class="text-2xl">
            The economic effects of network neutrality: a policy perspective
          </h3>
          <div class="flex flex-row gap-2">
            <p class="text-sm text-center">
              Eerola, Kasper. “The economic effects of network neutrality: a
              policy perspective.” (2015).
            </p>
            <a href="https://api.semanticscholar.org/CorpusID:56385185"
              ><span
                ><svg
                  xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"
                  width="24"
                  height="24"
                  fill="#000000"
                  viewBox="0 0 256 256"
                  ><path
                    d="M198,64V168a6,6,0,0,1-12,0V78.48L68.24,196.24a6,6,0,0,1-8.48-8.48L177.52,70H88a6,6,0,0,1,0-12H192A6,6,0,0,1,198,64Z"
                  ></path></svg
                ></span
              ></a
            >
          </div>
          <p>
            Description: This study investigates how the absence of net
            neutrality allows ISPs to implement "Zero-Rating" schemes that
            provide limited access to the internet for low-income users. The
            research argues that while this may seem helpful, it actually traps
            users in "Walled Gardens," preventing them from accessing the full
            educational and economic benefits of the open web. This not only
            exacerbates the digital divide but also stifles innovation and
            competition, ultimately harming the very users it claims to help.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="flex flex-col gap-4">
          <h3 class="text-2xl">
            The Digital Divide Is a Human Rights Issue: Advancing Social
            Inclusion Through Social Work Advocacy
          </h3>
          <div class="flex flex-row gap-2">
            <p class="text-sm">
              Sanders, C. K., & Scanlon, E. (2021). The Digital Divide Is a
              Human Rights Issue: Advancing Social Inclusion Through Social Work
              Advocacy. Journal of Human Rights and Social Work, 6, 130–143.
            </p>
            <a href="https://doi.org/10.1007/s41134-020-00147-9"
              ><span
                ><svg
                  xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"
                  width="24"
                  height="24"
                  fill="#000000"
                  viewBox="0 0 256 256"
                  ><path
                    d="M198,64V168a6,6,0,0,1-12,0V78.48L68.24,196.24a6,6,0,0,1-8.48-8.48L177.52,70H88a6,6,0,0,1,0-12H192A6,6,0,0,1,198,64Z"
                  ></path></svg
                ></span
              ></a
            >
          </div>
          <p>
            This paper argues that high-speed internet is a fundamental tool for
            participating in modern society, economy, and government. It
            explicitly identifies "digital redlining"—the practice where ISPs
            under-invest in or discriminate against low-income and minority
            communities—as a structural reality that perpetuates social and
            political disparities (Sanders & Scanlon, 2021). While the paper
            does not directly address net neutrality, it highlights how the lack
            of equitable internet access exacerbates the digital divide, which
            is a critical aspect of the broader net neutrality debate. The
            research emphasizes that without net neutrality protections, ISPs
            can further entrench digital redlining by prioritizing certain
            content and services, thereby deepening social inequalities.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="flex flex-col gap-4">
          <h3 class="text-2xl">
            Net Neutrality and its Implications to Online Learning
          </h3>
          <div class="flex flex-row gap-2">
            <p class="text-sm">
              Yamagata-Lynch, Lisa C., et al. “Net Neutrality and Its
              Implications to Online Learning”. The International Review of
              Research in Open and Distributed Learning, vol. 18, no. 6, Sept.
              2017, doi:10.19173/irrodl.v18i6.3129.
            </p>
            <a href="https://doi.org/10.19173/irrodl.v18i6.3129"
              ><span
                ><svg
                  xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"
                  width="24"
                  height="24"
                  fill="#000000"
                  viewBox="0 0 256 256"
                  ><path
                    d="M198,64V168a6,6,0,0,1-12,0V78.48L68.24,196.24a6,6,0,0,1-8.48-8.48L177.52,70H88a6,6,0,0,1,0-12H192A6,6,0,0,1,198,64Z"
                  ></path></svg
                ></span
              ></a
            >
          </div>
          <p>
            This research focuses on how the absence of net neutrality creates
            "tiered citizenship" in education. It finds that without these
            protections, a student's ability to succeed in online learning
            becomes dependent on their personal finances and whether they live
            in a "highly-populated city area or a less-populated rural area"
            (Yamagata-Lynch et al., 2017). The authors argue that net neutrality
            is essential for ensuring that students do not experience
            discrimination in service based on paid prioritization.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </main>
</Layout>
